# The repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf on, so anything git
# guesses is text gets its line endings rewritten on checkout. That guess is a
# heuristic (it looks for NUL bytes near the start of the file), and these
# files are flashed byte for byte — sbi_test.bin is loaded straight into the
# SPI flash model and koti.dtb is compared against dtc's output. A blob whose
# 0x0A bytes turned into 0x0D 0x0A boots nothing and diffs as "binary files
# differ", which says nothing about why.
*.bin binary
*.cpio binary
*.dtb binary
*.elf binary
*.fst binary
*.bit binary

# The rootfs overlay is copied VERBATIM into the target filesystem and then run
# by a Linux kernel, so the same autocrlf that is merely untidy elsewhere is
# fatal here: a shell script checked out with CRLF fails as
# `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`, and for /init specifically that is "no working
# init found" — a panic, on a machine whose whole point is that it boots.
# The committed blobs are already LF (git normalises on the way in) and CI
# checks out on Linux, so this guards the case where someone builds Buildroot
# from a Windows working tree or through WSL on /mnt/c.
sw/linux/rootfs-overlay/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.S text eol=lf

# ---- files whose CONTENT IS HASHED, so a line ending is a content change ----
# sw/linux/rootfs.provenance records the sha256 of the fragment and of every
# overlay file, and check_initramfs.py compares the working tree against it. On
# a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf those files come back with CRLF, the
# hashes differ from what CI built, and the failure reads as "the committed
# initramfs is stale" when nothing about it is. That cost two round trips on
# 2026-08-09 before the cause was spotted, both times mid-way through a build
# chain. Pinning them to LF makes the working tree and CI agree by construction.
sw/linux/buildroot_koti.fragment text eol=lf
sw/linux/rootfs-overlay/** text eol=lf
sw/linux/post-build.sh text eol=lf

# ⚠️ AFTER the rule above, because later rules win: the keymap lives inside the
# overlay and must NOT be touched. It is loaded byte for byte by `loadkmap`, and
# a 0x0A that became 0x0D 0x0A shifts every table after it -- every key past the
# damage then types the wrong character, on hardware, with nothing failing at
# build time.
*.bmap binary
